# __NAME__

A [rustango-cms](https://github.com/ujeenet/rustango-cms) site, generated with `rcms new`.

## Run

```sh
cargo run -- quickstart
```

The first run creates a local SQLite database in `./var`, a site called
`demo`, and an `admin` user (it asks you for a password of at least 12
characters), then starts the server. After that, `cargo run -- runserver`
is all you need. The commands are the same in bash, zsh and Windows
PowerShell; the database location comes from `.env`.

- Sign in: <http://demo.localhost:8080/login>
- Admin: <http://demo.localhost:8080/cms-admin/pages>
- Public pages: `http://demo.localhost:8080/<slug>/`

### What quickstart runs

The same steps by hand, if you want to name the site or its host yourself:

```sh
cargo run -- migrate-registry
cargo run -- create-tenant demo --mode database --database-url "sqlite:./var/demo.db?mode=rwc" --host-pattern demo.localhost
cargo run -- create-superuser demo admin
cargo run -- runserver
```

## Run on PostgreSQL

Build with the `postgres` feature and point `DATABASE_URL` at your
database: in `.env`, or in the shell.

```sh
# bash / zsh
createdb __NAME__
export DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/__NAME__
export RUSTANGO_APEX_DOMAIN=localtest.me   # tenant host = demo.localtest.me
```

```powershell
# Windows PowerShell
$env:DATABASE_URL = "postgres://localhost/__NAME__"
$env:RUSTANGO_APEX_DOMAIN = "localtest.me"
```

Then, in either shell:

```sh
cargo run --no-default-features --features postgres -- migrate-registry
cargo run --no-default-features --features postgres -- create-tenant demo
cargo run --no-default-features --features postgres -- create-superuser demo admin
cargo run --no-default-features --features postgres -- runserver
```

- Sign in: <http://demo.localtest.me:8080/login>
- Admin: <http://demo.localtest.me:8080/cms-admin/pages>
