## Deploy

Build once with `cargo build --release` (SQLite; add `--no-default-features --features postgres` for PostgreSQL), then on the server:

1. Copy the binary, `templates/` and `migrations/` to one folder, and set `RCMS_SITE_DIR` to it.
2. Set the production variables from `.env.example`: `RUSTANGO_ENV=prod`, `RUSTANGO_SESSION_SECRET`, `RUSTANGO_SECRET_KEY`, `RCMS_SECRET_KEY`, `RUSTANGO_APEX_DOMAIN`, `DATABASE_URL`.
3. On every deploy run `./__NAME__ migrate` before `./__NAME__ runserver`.
4. Put a reverse proxy with HTTPS in front, and back up the database and `./var/` (uploaded media, generated keys).

The full guide: <https://cms.rustango.com/production>.

## Layout

- `src/main.rs` — wires the admin + public router into the manage runner and seeds page types.
- `src/models.rs` — your page types (`HomePage`, `ArticlePage`). Add more with `register_page_type!` and a force-link line in `main.rs`.
- `templates/` — public page templates (the admin ships its own).
- `migrations/` — the CMS baseline migrations (registry, tenant, cms). Generate your own with the rustango migration tooling; never hand-edit these.

## Dependencies

`rustango-cms` and the `rustango` framework both come from crates.io. Keep `rustango` on the minor version that `rustango-cms` requires: two copies of the framework in one build give you `Tenant` and `Pool` types that do not match. (A project made with `rcms new --local` depends on a rustango-cms checkout by path instead.)
